  "description": [
   {
    "value": "Mark Twain (1835-1910), the famous American writer, earned a place in the history of Mormon literature as a humorist about nineteenth-century Mormon culture and as a minor critic of The Book of Mormon. In chapters 12-16 of Roughing It, Twain describes his visit to Utah and provides, among other commentary, a humorous defense of Mormon polygamy (Mormon women were so ugly that polygamy was an act of charity). Twain is (in)famous in Mormon circles for having said that the Book of Mormon is \"chlorof",
    "lang": "en",
    "via": "llm-page:Biography",
    "file": "P5469.json",
    "uri": false
   }
  ],
  "work": [
   {
    "value": "Roughing It",
    "lang": "en",
    "via": "llm-page:biography text",
    "file": "P5469.json",
    "uri": false
   },
   {
    "value": "The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn",
